course: myths
lesson: 11
title: Animals in the sea and the river
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: At the fair, Aunt Sue hands over a goldfish in a bag: "Don't worry, love, they've got a three-second memory, so it never gets bored going round and round!" It is a comfy story that makes a tiny bowl feel fine.
explain: But goldfish remember things for months; they can learn to come to a tap on the glass at feeding time and even push a lever for food. The myth was convenient, and convenient myths can hide unkindness.
explain: The water is full of tall tales like that: whales are fish, every shark dies if it stops, piranhas strip a cow in seconds, and a jellyfish sting is cured with wee, which comes from a TV comedy, not a lifeguard.
explain: Look closer and the truth is wilder: octopuses have three hearts and arms that do much of their thinking, seahorse dads give birth, and dolphins sleep with half their brain at a time.
explain: So underwater, check every claim: the strange ones are sometimes true, and the familiar ones are often wrong.
fact: Goldfish can remember things for months and can be trained to respond to sounds and push levers for food.
fact: Whales are mammals: they breathe air, give birth to live babies and feed them milk.
fact: Some sharks, like great whites, must keep swimming to breathe; others, like nurse sharks, can rest on the seabed and pump water over their gills.
fact: Weeing on a jellyfish sting does not help and can make it worse; rinse with seawater, remove tentacles carefully and get help from a lifeguard.
fact: Octopuses have eight arms, a central brain and three hearts, and much of their nervous system is in their arms.
fact: Piranhas mostly eat fish, insects and plants; attacks on people are rare and usually just nips, and the "cow in seconds" legend grew from a staged show with starved piranhas seen by US president Theodore Roosevelt.
fact: Fish have pain receptors (nerve ends that sense harm) and react to injury, and many experts think fish can feel pain.
fact: Seahorses are fish, and male seahorses carry the eggs in a pouch and give birth.
fact: Most crabs walk sideways because of how their legs bend, but some kinds can walk forwards.
fact: Dolphins rest one half of their brain at a time, so they can keep breathing and watching.
fact: Electric eels are not true eels but a kind of knifefish.
fact: Where an animal lives doesn't decide what kind of animal it is, and its name can be wrong.
exercise: Aunt Sue says goldfish have a three-second memory. What's true? | They remember things for months and can learn tricks | They forget absolutely everything after about three seconds | They remember only colours, not sounds | They have no memory at all
exercise: Jack says whales are fish. Why are they mammals? | They breathe air, give birth to live babies and feed them milk | They have fins and live in the sea | They lay eggs on the seabed | They breathe water through gills
exercise: Mike says all sharks must keep swimming or they die. What's true? | Some must, but nurse sharks can rest on the seabed | Every shark dies if it stops swimming for even a second | No shark ever needs to swim | Sharks breathe air at the surface
exercise: Jade says to wee on a jellyfish sting. What should you really do? | Rinse with seawater, remove tentacles carefully and get a lifeguard | Wee on it straight away | Rub it hard with sand | Pour cola on it and ignore it
exercise: Where did the "wee on a jellyfish sting" idea spread from? | A TV comedy scene and old tales | First-aid books written for lifeguards | A handbook that doctors use in hospital | Experts who study jellyfish for a living
exercise: Kian says octopuses have eight legs and one brain like us. What's even more amazing? | They have three hearts and much of their nerve system is in their arms | They have no brain at all | They have eight brains and no heart | They have two hearts and ten arms
exercise: Cerys says piranhas strip a cow in seconds. Where did that legend come from? | A staged show with starved piranhas, seen by Theodore Roosevelt | Daily attacks on cows in the Amazon | A famous nature film of a shoal of piranhas eating a whole whale | A scientist's careful field study
exercise: What do piranhas mostly eat? | Fish, insects and plants | Cows and horses that wade in | People who go swimming in rivers | Only other piranhas, nothing else
exercise: Uncle Fred says fish don't feel pain. What have scientists found? | Fish have pain receptors and react to injury | Fish have no nerves at all | Fish can feel only cold, nothing else at all | Fish feel pain only out of water
exercise: Megan says seahorse dads have the babies. Is it true? | Yes, males carry the eggs in a pouch and give birth | No, it's a myth from a cartoon | Only half true, because the mums carry them in a pouch | No, seahorses lay eggs on rocks and leave
exercise: Alfie says crabs can only walk sideways. What's true? | Most walk sideways, but some can walk forwards | All crabs everywhere can only ever walk sideways | Crabs only walk backwards, never any other way | Crabs can't really walk at all, they only swim about
exercise: Isla says dolphins sleep with half their brain at a time. True or myth? | True, so they can keep breathing and watching | Myth, dolphins never sleep | Myth, they sleep with both halves on the beach | True, but only baby dolphins
exercise: Sami reads "electric eel" on a label. What is it really? | A kind of knifefish, not a true eel | A real eel that eats batteries for food | A type of glowing jellyfish from the deep | A sea snake that lives in warm water
exercise: Why did the three-second memory myth spread so easily? | It made people feel fine about keeping fish in tiny bowls | Scientists proved it in a lab | Goldfish never learn anything | Fairs printed it on every plastic bag of goldfish they gave away
exercise: What's a way to test a goldfish's memory at home? | Tap the glass at every feeding and see if it learns to come | Ask it questions out loud | Leave it in the dark for a year | Change all its water every three seconds and see if it notices
test: "Whales live in the sea, so they're fish, obviously." What's the flaw? | Where an animal lives doesn't decide what it is | Nothing, sea animals are all fish | Whales live on land too | Fish breathe air too, so there's no difference at all
test: In a sitcom, a friend wees on someone's jellyfish sting and it's cured. What would a lifeguard say? | Wee can make it worse; rinse with seawater and remove tentacles | That's the best first aid there is | Only fresh wee works | Wee works on jellyfish stings and on shark bites as well, so go for it
test: You see a shark lying still on the seabed in an aquarium. Is it dead? | Probably not; some sharks like nurse sharks rest and pump water over their gills | Yes, every still shark is dead | Yes, sharks can't breathe unless swimming | It's asleep with both eyes shut, like us
test: "Goldie's fine in that jam jar, she forgets it in 3 secs lol." How do you reply kindly? | "They can learn tricks and remember for months! Maybe she'd like a bigger tank." | "You're cruel and I'm telling everyone." | "True, so it doesn't matter." | "Jars are better than tanks."
test: What would happen if a dolphin fell fully asleep with its whole brain, like we do? | It might forget to come up to breathe | It would sleep better and live longer | Nothing at all would change | It would sink and breathe water
test: Your uncle says fishing doesn't hurt fish because "they don't scream". What's the fair answer? | Not showing pain like us isn't the same as not feeling it | He's right, no scream means no pain | Fish scream underwater all the time, where people can't hear them | Only big fish feel pain
test: A film shows a cow lowered into a river and eaten by piranhas in ten seconds. What's really true? | That kind of scene is a legend from a staged show; real piranhas rarely attack | It happens every day in rivers | Piranhas only eat cows, never fish | Piranhas live in British rivers too
test: Megan says, "Bet you don't believe seahorse dads give birth!" What's the wise reaction? | Check it, and admit it's true | Laugh it off without ever checking | Say only mammal dads can do that | Say all fish dads do that, so it's nothing special
test: An aquarium label says "Electric eel" but the guide says it isn't an eel. What's the lesson? | Names can be wrong; the animal is a knifefish | The guide must be lying | Eels change species when electric | Labels are always more correct than tour guides
test: A crab on the beach walks straight forwards. Your mate says "impossible, crabs ONLY go sideways". What do you say? | Most go sideways, but some kinds walk forwards | He's right, you must be dreaming | That crab must be broken | Crabs walk forwards only at night when nobody's looking
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the classification of whales? | They are mammals, breathing air and nourishing their young with milk | They are fish, by reason of their marine habitat | They are amphibians, living both in and out of the water, as frogs do | They are reptiles possessing fins
exam: It is given that a bather has been stung by a jellyfish. Select the response consistent with established first-aid advice. | Rinsing with seawater, careful removal of tentacles and summoning a lifeguard | Application of urine to the affected area | Vigorous rubbing of the area with sand | Disregard of the sting until the following day
exam: Identify the parent which, among seahorses, carries the eggs and gives birth. | The male | The female | Both parents equally | Neither, the eggs being abandoned upon rocks
exam: Ascertain which of the following claims concerning the goldfish is consistent with experimental findings. | It is capable of retaining learned behaviour for months | Its memory is limited to a period of approximately three seconds | It is incapable of learning to respond to sounds | It retains memories solely of colours
